WEBVTT

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:10.830
When a collection holds seventy-two self-directed courses and
hundreds of other resources, the hardest part can be knowing where

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.626
to start.

00:00:11.748 --> 00:00:14.506
The Core 15 answers that question.

00:00:14.628 --> 00:00:21.820
It is a set of fifteen courses chosen for disability services work,
gathered near the top of the Learn page so you can find them

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.570
quickly.

00:00:22.692 --> 00:00:28.770
They are recommendations rather than required training, and you can
take them in any order and at your own pace.

00:00:29.337 --> 00:00:36.392
Each of these courses is built around situations people in the
division actually meet, in services and in the workplace.

00:00:36.514 --> 00:00:44.160
Together they move in three steps: a shared foundation, equity
applied to real decisions, and the systems view a leader needs.

00:00:44.282 --> 00:00:47.458
The groups also follow the course levels.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:55.224
Start here draws on Foundation courses, Apply it on Intermediate
ones, and Lead it on Advanced ones, which is why the set makes a

00:00:55.224 --> 00:00:57.302
strong first year of learning.

00:00:57.869 --> 00:01:02.309
The first six courses give you and your colleagues a common
language.

00:01:02.431 --> 00:01:10.654
An Equity Lens for Ordinary Decisions offers six questions about
purpose, participation, requirements, access, evidence and

00:01:10.654 --> 00:01:11.823
consequences.

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:19.001
Other courses explore cultural humility, unconscious bias, and
different ways of understanding disability and belonging.

00:01:19.123 --> 00:01:25.929
The last two look at accessibility in everyday services and work,
and at language access, including the difference between

00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:30.078
translation, interpretation and checking that someone has
understood.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.433
These are the habits every later course relies on.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.893
The next six take that language into real decisions.

00:01:38.016 --> 00:01:45.348
You choose communication for the person and the task, support
informed choice in person-centered planning, and work a realistic

00:01:45.348 --> 00:01:48.247
decision through the Equity Analysis Toolkit.

00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:56.140
You also practice engagement that is honest about what people can
actually shape, hiring that rests on relevant evidence rather than

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:02.091
credentials that simply feel familiar, and responding to
microaggressions with care and purpose.

00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:08.768
Each is built around real service and workplace situations, with
room for reflection and practice.

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:12.568
The final three widen the view to systems.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:20.487
From Individual Access Fixes to Accessible Systems asks you to map
barriers across a whole journey, not a single document.

00:02:20.609 --> 00:02:28.694
Putting Three ADSA Equity Goals into Practice helps you explain the
six goals of the Aging and Disability Services Administration and

00:02:28.694 --> 00:02:31.487
connect three of them to real conditions.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:38.753
Policy and Legislative Analysis through an Equity Lens works with
real Minnesota policy and legislative records.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.198
Notice how the groups build on one another.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:50.456
Accessibility in everyday work becomes accessible communication for
one person and one task, and then becomes the design of accessible

00:02:50.456 --> 00:02:51.242
systems.

00:02:51.364 --> 00:02:59.160
The equity lens for ordinary decisions grows into a full toolkit
analysis, and then into policy and legislative work.

00:02:59.282 --> 00:03:05.538
Learning about unconscious bias prepares you for fairer hiring and
for responding well in a hard moment.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.893
Each step asks a little more of the same skills.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:17.681
The Core 15 are drawn from the One DSD Self-Directed Learning
Series, so when a course sparks a new question, the full series of

00:03:17.681 --> 00:03:19.716
seventy-two is right below.

00:03:19.838 --> 00:03:26.829
You can choose Foundation, Intermediate or Advanced courses, and
download editable slides to use with a team.

00:03:26.951 --> 00:03:33.206
Working through a course with colleagues can turn its questions into
a useful conversation about your shared work.

00:03:33.328 --> 00:03:40.632
The slides you download are separate copies on your device, and
taking part does not by itself establish official training credit.

00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:49.262
Learning matters most when it reaches a real decision, which is why
the Learn page points you onward to the thirteen guided practices.

00:03:49.384 --> 00:03:57.365
Each practice is built around a real task, so a course on fair
recruitment, for example, has a natural partner in the practice for

00:03:57.365 --> 00:03:59.568
inclusive hiring and selection.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.840
Nothing here is graded, and My work keeps your place in the courses
on your own computer.

00:04:04.962 --> 00:04:12.018
For a next step, choose one Start here course that speaks to
something on your desk this week, and give it an unhurried hour.
